WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:10.824
This is Chapter 5: workload identity
federation and managed identities.

4
00:00:10.824 --> 00:00:15.463
It's where service accounts actually
get converted: a managed identity first,

5
00:00:15.463 --> 00:00:18.362
then a federated credential,
then a certificate,

6
00:00:18.362 --> 00:00:22.275
with a thirty-day parallel run
before the old account is disabled.

7
00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:26.978
We'll start from Microsoft's own samples,
see what they accept in silence,

8
00:00:26.978 --> 00:00:29.927
then walk the checks,
the token exchange test,

9
00:00:29.927 --> 00:00:33.275
the plan and its write path,
two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:38.800
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.275
Why does this chapter exist?

12
00:00:44.275 --> 00:00:46.112
Because of one quiet failure.

13
00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:50.637
A federated credential with the wrong
subject is created without any error.

14
00:00:50.637 --> 00:00:55.016
Nothing complains until the day the
workload tries to exchange its token,

15
00:00:55.016 --> 00:00:58.462
and for GitHub, Learn says even
that fails without an error.

16
00:00:58.462 --> 00:01:01.201
The plan's risk register
names exactly this,

17
00:01:01.201 --> 00:01:05.688
and its answer is to test every exchange
before the parallel run starts.

18
00:01:05.688 --> 00:01:08.025
That test is the center of the chapter.

19
00:01:08.025 --> 00:01:10.708
The order of conversion comes from Book 2:

20
00:01:10.708 --> 00:01:15.285
managed identities where the host
is in Azure, federated credentials

21
00:01:15.285 --> 00:01:19.980
where the host speaks O I D C,
certificates only where neither works.

22
00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.093
Next, which cloud.

23
00:01:25.093 --> 00:01:30.064
This chapter talks to three hosts:
Microsoft Graph for app registrations and

24
00:01:30.064 --> 00:01:35.268
sign-ins, Resource Manager for managed
identities, and the sign-in host for tokens.

25
00:01:35.268 --> 00:01:38.721
The estate is a G C C tenant
with commercial Azure,

26
00:01:38.721 --> 00:01:41.656
so all three are the worldwide endpoints.

27
00:01:41.656 --> 00:01:45.782
G C C High and the Defense Department
clouds have their own hosts,

28
00:01:45.782 --> 00:01:48.118
and the code switches only for those.

29
00:01:48.118 --> 00:01:52.882
One gap worth knowing: the managed identity
pages the chapter read don't say whether

30
00:01:52.882 --> 00:01:55.091
they're available in Azure Government,

31
00:01:55.091 --> 00:01:58.900
which is fine here because this
estate's Azure is commercial.

32
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.487
So what is a federated credential?

33
00:02:04.487 --> 00:02:09.099
It's a trust you put on an app registration
or a user-assigned managed identity.

34
00:02:09.099 --> 00:02:12.862
The workload, say a GitHub
workflow or a Kubernetes pod,

35
00:02:12.862 --> 00:02:18.296
presents a token from its own issuer, and
Microsoft Entra swaps it for an access

36
00:02:18.296 --> 00:02:23.563
token, but only if three values match:
the issuer, the subject and the audience.

37
00:02:23.563 --> 00:02:27.900
The match is exact, down to the
letter case, with no wildcards.

38
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.012
A system-assigned identity
can't hold one at all.

39
00:02:31.012 --> 00:02:34.203
And managed identities, the
first choice in the order,

40
00:02:34.203 --> 00:02:39.000
get their access from Azure role
assignments, which this chapter records.

41
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.462
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

42
00:02:44.462 --> 00:02:48.441
Two Learn pages walk through
creating a federated credential,

43
00:02:48.441 --> 00:02:53.033
one for an app registration and one
for a user-assigned managed identity,

44
00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:57.624
with samples in the command line,
PowerShell, a template and raw requests.

45
00:02:57.624 --> 00:03:00.180
Graph's reference has
its own create request,

46
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.425
and the managed identity
reference has worked examples.

47
00:03:03.425 --> 00:03:08.143
All of that lives only inside web pages,
so the repository pins the sources

48
00:03:08.143 --> 00:03:10.541
unmodified, with hashes a test checks,

49
00:03:10.541 --> 00:03:13.712
and a weekly watch that
reports upstream changes.

50
00:03:13.712 --> 00:03:16.692
A harness then runs every
sample against fakes,

51
00:03:16.692 --> 00:03:19.500
so you can see what each one really does.

52
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.012
This is the heart of the chapter.

53
00:03:25.012 --> 00:03:29.549
Run against the fakes, Microsoft's
samples show the risk on their own terms.

54
00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:32.850
Graph happily creates a
credential with any subject.

55
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.263
Learn's samples don't even
agree on the GitHub issuer:

56
00:03:36.263 --> 00:03:40.550
one has a trailing slash and the
others don't, and the match is exact.

57
00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:44.328
One template defaults the audience
with a stray leading space,

58
00:03:44.328 --> 00:03:45.563
so it can never match.

59
00:03:45.563 --> 00:03:49.438
And two Learn pages give two
different pull request subjects.

60
00:03:49.438 --> 00:03:53.976
So the code checks each credential against
what the workload will really present,

61
00:03:53.976 --> 00:03:57.270
and derives a subject only
when the format is certain.

62
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.257
The rest of the gap table is
about everything around the write.

63
00:04:04.257 --> 00:04:08.185
The samples hard-code worldwide
hosts, use a preview version,

64
00:04:08.185 --> 00:04:11.483
and in one case send a request
with no version at all.

65
00:04:11.483 --> 00:04:13.947
They create one credential
at a time by hand,

66
00:04:13.947 --> 00:04:17.370
so nothing notices a credential
someone added in the portal.

67
00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:19.908
They don't check the limits
Learn itself states.

68
00:04:19.908 --> 00:04:24.168
And they stop at creation: no
record of the identity's roles,

69
00:04:24.168 --> 00:04:26.121
no parallel run, no disable.

70
00:04:26.121 --> 00:04:29.926
The verdict: use the samples as
the model for the three writes,

71
00:04:29.926 --> 00:04:35.010
but not as the conversion, because they
accept the wrong subject in silence.

72
00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.085
So what does the code add?

73
00:04:40.085 --> 00:04:42.522
A single new module and one entry script,

74
00:04:42.522 --> 00:04:47.322
which builds a plan by default and only
writes when you hand it an approved plan.

75
00:04:47.322 --> 00:04:51.809
It reuses the shared cloud table and
hashing from the earlier chapters

76
00:04:51.809 --> 00:04:55.686
but keeps its own client and
fakes, so nothing earlier changes.

77
00:04:55.686 --> 00:04:56.947
Two files drive it.

78
00:04:56.947 --> 00:05:01.723
The conversion file, kept by identity
engineering and reviewed with the application

79
00:05:01.723 --> 00:05:04.481
owners, says what each
legacy credential becomes

80
00:05:04.481 --> 00:05:06.835
and which workload will present the token.

81
00:05:06.835 --> 00:05:09.107
And the cutover log, kept by the owner,

82
00:05:09.107 --> 00:05:13.640
records when each workload switched
and who approved the disable.

83
00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:19.727
Here are the nine rules
the planner applies.

84
00:05:19.727 --> 00:05:23.797
Before any of them, it reads
each app registration, identity,

85
00:05:23.797 --> 00:05:29.028
role assignment and legacy account, plus
sign-ins once a cutover is recorded.

86
00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:34.257
Most rules come straight from Learn: the
subject, issuer and audience must match

87
00:05:34.257 --> 00:05:39.565
exactly, names must follow the rules, and
no parent may go past twenty credentials.

88
00:05:39.565 --> 00:05:44.276
Three are the program's own and labeled
that way: the conversion order, a warning

89
00:05:44.276 --> 00:05:48.915
for credentials nobody reviewed, and
the thresholds for a clean parallel run.

90
00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:52.150
And the failed exchange
test is its own finding.

91
00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:57.737
Two pieces make the check real.

92
00:05:57.737 --> 00:06:02.462
First, the code works out what the workload's
issuer will actually put in its token.

93
00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:06.297
For GitHub it uses Learn's
three formats, for environments,

94
00:06:06.297 --> 00:06:10.612
branches and tags, and for Kubernetes
the service account format.

95
00:06:10.612 --> 00:06:13.733
Anything it can't be sure
of, like a pull request,

96
00:06:13.733 --> 00:06:18.662
or GitHub's newer immutable format, has
to be captured from a real test run.

97
00:06:18.662 --> 00:06:21.990
Second, it builds the real
token request and sends it

98
00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:25.550
to a fake endpoint that matches
the way Learn describes.

99
00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:28.722
It doesn't check signatures,
so it proves the credential

100
00:06:28.722 --> 00:06:32.050
matches what you recorded,
not what the issuer sends.

101
00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.350
Nothing in the plan writes.

102
00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:43.008
Each conversion gets actions, and only three
of them ever write: create an identity,

103
00:06:43.008 --> 00:06:47.750
create a credential, and disable the
legacy account, all after review.

104
00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:50.409
Everything else is a record or a status,

105
00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:53.650
from awaiting cutover to
day fifteen of thirty.

106
00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:56.744
A changed credential is
never edited in place;

107
00:06:56.744 --> 00:06:59.512
it gets a new name and
its own parallel run.

108
00:06:59.512 --> 00:07:03.998
The evidence for a clean run comes from
the beta sign-in log, the only place that

109
00:07:03.998 --> 00:07:08.975
shows managed identity and service principal
sign-ins and which credential was used.

110
00:07:08.975 --> 00:07:14.190
The plan is hashed, so the same inputs
give the same plan, byte for byte.

111
00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:22.015
Writing is a separate path, and
it's deliberately hard to misuse.

112
00:07:22.015 --> 00:07:25.833
It runs only against a plan
whose hash a reviewer approved,

113
00:07:25.833 --> 00:07:29.015
for this tenant and cloud,
and no older than a week.

114
00:07:29.015 --> 00:07:33.046
Every credential is checked again
against the plan's recorded claims,

115
00:07:33.046 --> 00:07:37.077
so a hand-edited plan can't sneak
through, and it never deletes anything.

116
00:07:37.077 --> 00:07:40.091
It runs as a person,
not an unattended app.

117
00:07:40.091 --> 00:07:44.130
Before each write it looks again,
and it sends one write at a time,

118
00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:49.102
identity first, because Learn says
parallel creates under one identity fail.

119
00:07:49.102 --> 00:07:53.450
A new credential also needs a few
minutes to propagate before it works.

120
00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:58.262
Who is this for?

121
00:07:58.262 --> 00:07:59.650
Developers build it.

122
00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:03.550
Identity engineers run the waves
and keep the conversion file.

123
00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:06.920
And the application owners
switch each workload,

124
00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:10.125
keep the cutover log, and
approve every disable.

125
00:08:10.125 --> 00:08:11.937
The access is split in two.

126
00:08:11.937 --> 00:08:16.174
The read path is an app with read-only
permissions and the Reader role.

127
00:08:16.174 --> 00:08:20.135
The write path is only ever a person,
signed in with their own roles,

128
00:08:20.135 --> 00:08:23.947
because a standing app with the
right to add credentials to any

129
00:08:23.947 --> 00:08:27.687
application is exactly what this
program is trying to remove.

130
00:08:27.687 --> 00:08:29.516
For the identity's credential,

131
00:08:29.516 --> 00:08:33.450
the code picks the narrower
built-in role over Contributor.

132
00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:43.338
Lab 1 needs only a laptop with PowerShell
7 and Pester; no tenant, no network.

133
00:08:43.338 --> 00:08:46.713
First you run Microsoft's own
samples against the fakes.

134
00:08:46.713 --> 00:08:51.206
Find the test that creates a credential
with Graph's sample and then exchanges

135
00:08:51.206 --> 00:08:55.562
a token with a different subject: the
create succeeds and the exchange fails.

136
00:08:55.562 --> 00:08:57.925
That's the whole risk, in one test.

137
00:08:57.925 --> 00:09:02.290
Then you list what each task needs,
and build the plan from the fixtures:

138
00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:05.902
eight conversions in two waves,
two failed exchange tests,

139
00:09:05.902 --> 00:09:08.987
and twenty actions, of
which only four would write.

140
00:09:08.987 --> 00:09:12.390
The plan's hash is the same on every run.

141
00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.078
Now the fixtures tell their stories.

142
00:09:18.078 --> 00:09:22.871
One credential copies the sample's
trailing slash and gets the environment's

143
00:09:22.871 --> 00:09:26.840
letter case wrong, so it fails
three ways, including the exchange.

144
00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.302
Another copies the template's
stray space in the audience.

145
00:09:30.302 --> 00:09:33.828
A credential someone added in
the portal shows up as a warning.

146
00:09:33.828 --> 00:09:37.786
One conversion has run forty clean
days with the owner's approval,

147
00:09:37.786 --> 00:09:39.985
so it's the single disable in the plan,

148
00:09:39.985 --> 00:09:44.090
while another's old secret was still
signing in weeks after cutover.

149
00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:48.087
Then you dry-run with the approved
hash, apply to the fake in order,

150
00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:51.240
and watch a hash nobody
approved get refused.

151
00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:58.240
Lab 2 moves to a test
tenant, never production.

152
00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:01.852
You set up one small conversion:
a test app with a secret,

153
00:10:01.852 --> 00:10:06.990
a test service account, and a test
repository whose workflow signs in to Azure.

154
00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.182
The key step is capturing the claims:

155
00:10:09.182 --> 00:10:13.273
have the workflow print the decoded
issuer and subject of its token,

156
00:10:13.273 --> 00:10:17.802
never the token itself, because that's
the truth the check compares against.

157
00:10:17.802 --> 00:10:21.113
Then plan, get a second
person to approve the hash,

158
00:10:21.113 --> 00:10:24.753
apply, and watch a real exchange
land in the sign-in log.

159
00:10:24.753 --> 00:10:26.873
Try a wrong subject on purpose,

160
00:10:26.873 --> 00:10:31.050
run the parallel run forward,
and back it all out by hand.

161
00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.275
Each of these dangers
has a guard in the code.

162
00:10:37.275 --> 00:10:39.818
The headline one is
still the wrong subject,

163
00:10:39.818 --> 00:10:43.775
caught by the claims check and the
exchange test before any cutover.

164
00:10:43.775 --> 00:10:47.263
Others are small: a trailing
slash, a stray space,

165
00:10:47.263 --> 00:10:50.125
a subject guessed instead of captured.

166
00:10:50.125 --> 00:10:54.038
Some are about timing, like
creating credentials in parallel,

167
00:10:54.038 --> 00:10:56.263
or using one before it's propagated.

168
00:10:56.263 --> 00:11:00.149
And some are about keeping your
way back: the code never deletes,

169
00:11:00.149 --> 00:11:03.812
so the rollback, re-enabling
the old account, always remains.

170
00:11:03.812 --> 00:11:07.710
Notice that writes stay with a
person, never a standing app.

171
00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:12.810
Here's what done looks like.

172
00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:15.760
Every conversion followed
the order or says why.

173
00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:20.600
Every federated credential was checked
against claims captured from a real run,

174
00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:23.273
and passed its exchange
test, before cutover.

175
00:11:23.273 --> 00:11:26.048
Nothing sits in the tenant
that nobody reviewed.

176
00:11:26.048 --> 00:11:29.710
The plan was approved by someone
other than the person who applied it.

177
00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:34.864
Every parallel run had thirty clean days,
every disable has the owner's approval,

178
00:11:34.864 --> 00:11:38.222
and nothing was deleted, so
the way back is still open.

179
00:11:38.222 --> 00:11:41.755
Next, Chapter 6 turns to group
managed service accounts,

180
00:11:41.755 --> 00:11:45.672
delegated managed service
accounts, and the Kerberos island.

181
00:11:45.672 --> 00:11:46.830
Thanks for watching.
